<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Realm of the Gyulas</h1><p class="st-meta">950 CE – 1003 CE (approx.) · capital Gyulafehérvár</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Semi-independent realm of the gyulas in Transylvania, whose Orthodox-leaning rulers were subdued by Stephen I of Hungary in 1003.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Hungarian conquest, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a> was held by a branch of chiefs bearing the title gyula. Byzantine sources record that a gyula was baptised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> around 952 and brought back the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hierotheos">Hierotheos</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Their seat was probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a> (Alba Iulia), on a Roman fort.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The gyulas ruled largely independently of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> grand princes, controlling Transylvanian salt and the routes to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>. Their daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarolt">Sarolt</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geza-grand-prince-of-the-hungarians">Grand Prince Géza</a> and was the mother of Stephen I. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was oriented to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, unlike the Western church favoured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1003 Stephen I attacked his maternal uncle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyula-the-younger">Gyula the Younger</a>, captured him and annexed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary</a> (1000-1301). The region later became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/voivodeship-of-transylvania">Voivodeship of Transylvania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 950 CE – 1003 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.61%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 CE – 1003 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gyulafehérvár</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gyula the Elder, Gyula the Younger</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Hungarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gyula's Transylvania, Duchy of Gyula</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=976&amp;focus=realm-of-the-gyulas" aria-label="Realm of the Gyulas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5256 572 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Realm of the Gyulas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5446 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5576 828h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5576 828h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Romania. <a href="/atlas?year=976&amp;focus=realm-of-the-gyulas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

